package org.mattshoe.mtg.core

internal val storyOmenpaths = StorySection(
    "omenpaths", "Omenpath Era",
    "When the Phyrexians fell they left the doors between worlds hanging open, and for the first time in living memory anyone at all could walk through them.",
    listOf(
        StoryArticle(
            "the-omenpaths", "The Omenpaths",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "kaldheims-roads", "Kaldheim's Roads",
                    listOf(
                        "Before the rest of the Multiverse had ever heard the word, the Omenpaths belonged to a single plane. Kaldheim was ten realms hung among the branches of one cosmic World Tree, and between those realms lay the Cosmos, a dark and maddening gulf that swallowed most who tried to cross it. The realms were not fixed. They drifted on the tree, and as they drifted, passages sometimes opened from one to another. The people of Kaldheim called these passages Omenpaths, because an opening was rarely good news.",
                        "Some were nothing more than a shimmer of light across a meadow that a farmer could step through without breaking stride. Others were tunnels that shifted under a traveller's hands and spat them out on the peak of a mountain in a realm they had never seen. A few powerful mages learned to close them and open them again at will. Often an Omenpath was only the first sign of something worse, a Doomskar, when two realms ground against each other and their peoples went to war.",
                        "Every other way across the Cosmos was hard. The gods, made what they were by the Cosmos Elixir, could pass between realms almost as they pleased. The Omenseekers sailed it in longboats carved with runes. Even a planeswalker could not simply step from one realm to the next, and Kaya needed Cosima's longboat to cross, while the trickster Tibalt cut his way through with the Sword of the Realms. On Kaldheim the paths were a local wonder and a local danger, and nobody imagined them reaching any further.",
                        "Then a Phyrexian came hunting. The [praetor](praetors) Vorinclex stalked Kaldheim's realms for prey worthy of him, and when he left he carried away tyrite from the World Tree itself. Nobody on Kaldheim understood what had been taken. On New Phyrexia, deep in the plane's innermost sphere, somebody did.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-invasion-tree", "The Invasion Tree",
                    listOf(
                        "In the Seedcore, the incubation chamber at the heart of New Phyrexia, the stolen tyrite was planted and something grew. A cosmic tree ought to have torn the plane apart from the inside, but [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn) found a way to steady its growth. It was called Realmbreaker. In its early days it was a sapling with beads of glistening oil hanging from its twigs like buds, and Norn meant to use it to carry New Phyrexia into every world there was.",
                        "A small strike force of planeswalkers came to destroy it before it could. They carried the Filigree Sylex, a perfect replica of the bomb that had once ended the [Brothers' War](sylex-blast), built by Saheeli Rai. They came too late. Nissa Revane had already been compleated, and she had driven the tree to full size. Its porcelain bark rose through the spheres until its branches pierced the gardens above, and from the tips of those branches it had begun to open Omenpaths of its own. Inside its roots they found [Karn](who/karn), cut open and spread across a dais, still able to speak, urging them to set the bomb off. They hesitated, afraid it might take every connected world with it, and the chance passed.",
                        "When the [invasion](invasion-of-the-multiverse) came, {Realmbreaker, the Invasion Tree} was its spear. Its branches tore through the [Blind Eternities](the-blind-eternities) and anchored in plane after plane with three-pronged barbs. Thousands of spiny seedpods rode those branches across the void, each carrying soldiers of the Machine Legion, and the branches themselves crushed cities and pulled warships out of the sky.",
                        "It was turned in the end by a planeswalker who had lost everything. Wrenn, a dryad whose bonded tree had died in the fighting, bonded with Realmbreaker instead and gave it a new name, Eight. Together, as {Wrenn and Realmbreaker}, they reached the lost plane of Zhalfir and swapped its place in the Multiverse with New Phyrexia's. As she worked, the branches pulled back, but the Omenpaths they had punched open did not close. The angels of New Capenna flew out through them to fight for other worlds. Wrenn died of the strain, her body turned to ash at the tree's root, and the tree's own fate is unknown. The paths it had made outlived them both.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-sparks-go-out", "The Sparks Go Out",
                    listOf(
                        "Something else happened in the weeks after the invasion, and at first hardly anyone noticed. Planeswalkers began to lose their sparks. It was not sudden and it was not violent. The spark simply faded, so quietly that some of them only found out when they reached for the Blind Eternities and touched nothing. The world they happened to be standing on became the only world they would ever see again. Many had gone home to defend their own planes during the invasion, so most were at least trapped among their own people.",
                        "This was the event that came to be called the Desparkening, and it was nothing like the way [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) had harvested sparks in the [War of the Spark](bolas-war-of-the-spark). Nobody died of it. Those who lost a spark kept their souls and their lives, and described what was left behind as a hole, like a missing limb that had somehow been sunburnt. The Wanderer of Kamigawa, who had spent her life fighting to stay anchored in one place, lost hers and found peace in it. Others took it far worse. The [Sparks Lost](sparks-lost) article tells who.",
                        "Nobody knows why it happened. [Teferi](who/teferi) suspected the Multiverse was trying to heal itself after two great wounds, Elspeth's detonation of {The Filigree Sylex} inside the Blind Eternities and the tearing swap of Zhalfir for New Phyrexia. Zimone Wola, a dimensional researcher from Strixhaven, argued from a theory called the Vorzani Conjecture that planeswalkers had once served as observers who held the Multiverse in shape, and that the Omenpaths had simply made them unnecessary. Saheeli Rai reasoned that mana and aether cannot be destroyed, only moved, and that the missing sparks had therefore gone somewhere.",
                        "None of these explanations has been proved. All that is certain is the timing. The sparks went out across the Multiverse at the same moment the doors between worlds stayed open, and a handful of walkers, Ral Zarek and Oko among them, kept theirs while almost everyone else lost theirs. The planeswalkers had been the only people who could travel between worlds. Now they were nearly gone, and everyone else could.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "roads-without-maps", "Roads Without Maps",
                    listOf(
                        "The new Omenpaths were not like Kaldheim's round ones. These were triangles, or swirling sets of interlocking triangles, said to be the strongest shape for holding up the weight of the Multiverse. Nissa saw one on Zhalfir as a hovering blue light that pulsed to the song of the leylines, its edges ragged like cloth cut by a careless blade. On Eldraine a boy named Kellan found one standing free in the woods like a cloudy mirror. On Ixalan they hung as sparkling rings wider than a person, floating above the ground and full of turning light.",
                        "They came in every size and temper. Some were tiny and some were vast. Some stayed and some vanished. Some wandered across the land, some led only one way, and a traveller who stepped through had no promise of ever finding a road back. Not every plane connected to every other, so a journey could take many crossings. For the first time since [the Mending](the-mending) shut down every planar machine in existence, ordinary people could leave their worlds, and they did, in their thousands.",
                        "People began to tame them. Saheeli tracked down Ixalan from elsewhere in less than a year. A path between Strixhaven on Arcavios and Ixalan proved regular enough for couriers to run letters back and forth. The planners of Avishkar, the world once called Kaladesh, sorted the paths into three plain grades. Evergreen paths had opened in the invasion's wake and never closed. Deciduous paths opened and shut on a reliable cycle, like seasons. Instanced paths were everything else, from one that stayed open for days to one that lasted a few seconds. The scheme was never meant for scholars, but it spread across the Multiverse anyway.",
                        "Kellan was the proof of what had changed. He was half fae, the son of the planeswalker Oko, and he had no spark at all, yet he walked from Eldraine to Ixalan to Ravnica looking for the father he had never met, and every step was through an Omenpath. The card that shows him, {Kellan, Planar Trailblazer}, names what he had become, a traveller between worlds with no spark to carry him. Nobody needed to be special to cross the Multiverse any more. They only needed to be brave, or desperate, or greedy, and the Multiverse was about to find out how many people were all three.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "a-crowded-multiverse", "A Crowded Multiverse",
                    listOf(
                        "The worlds that had been alone were alone no longer. On Innistrad, where scholars had long assumed their plane was all of creation, the paths stirred a new hunger to study what lay beyond. Peddlers began to hawk spices from Naktamun on Amonkhet and cookware from Fiora as if both were down the road. Settlers poured onto the empty plains of [Thunder Junction](thunder-junction), and outlaws followed close behind them.",
                        "The great powers saw the paths as a contest. On Ravnica, Niv-Mizzet judged the shifting patterns of the Omenpaths a threat to his plane, and in secret he set agents across every guild to study them. His Omenpath Project was uncovered in the middle of a string of murders, told in [Karlov Manor](karlov-manor), and by its end the Firemind meant to make Ravnica the hub of the whole Multiverse. He and Ral Zarek built relay pylons that could carry messages through the paths, the first lines of talk between worlds. Avishkar, blessed with a crowd of stable paths around its capital, opened embassies on other planes and named Ravnica its chief rival, then showed off its reach with a race that ran across several worlds, told in [Aetherdrift](aetherdrift).",
                        "Not every door led somewhere kind. A demon named Valgavoth learned to disguise his own portals as harmless doors, and people from Kamigawa and Ravnica stepped through them into the House of [Duskmourn](duskmourn) and did not come back. When the dragonstorms of Tarkir went wild, dragons began bursting through the paths onto other worlds, and each beat of their wings tore at the Omenpaths themselves, which is the story of [Tarkir Dragonstorm](tarkir-dragonstorm).",
                        "Some of the old planeswalkers looked at all of this with dread. [Jace Beleren](who/jace), who still had his spark, and Vraska, who had lost hers, stood before an Omenpath on Jace's home plane of Vryn and agreed that it should not exist at all. The Gatewatch had been founded to stop threats that were stuck on one world, and now a warlord could walk away from the world that had beaten him and start again on the next. Their answer began in a buried vault on Thunder Junction, where a strange child slept who could see the end of every Omenpath at once.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Can I interest you in some herbs from Naktamun? Fresh from the Omenpath! Or a set of Fioran cookware? Finest in the Multiverse!",
                        "Discerning Peddler",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Kaldheim"),
                StorySource("Set", "Phyrexia: All Will Be One"),
                StorySource("Set", "March of the Machine"),
                StorySource("Set", "March of the Machine: The Aftermath"),
                StorySource("Story", "March of the Machine, Episode 8: Wrenn and Eight, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "March of the Machine: The Aftermath, She Who Breaks the World, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Murders at Karlov Manor, Episode 11: Portents and Omens, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Aetherdrift, Parts 1 and 2, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Omenpath"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Realmbreaker"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Desparkening"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "thunder-junction", "Thunder Junction",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "omenport", "Omenport",
                    listOf(
                        "Before the [Omenpaths](the-omenpaths) opened, nobody lived on Thunder Junction. It was a desert plane of red canyons, cactus and tumbleweed under a huge sky, and the only things on it older than the paths were a few carved obelisks and a vault that most people would come to call a myth. Then the doors opened and the Multiverse poured in. Settlers came looking for land, and outlaws came looking for settlers. The opening of the paths woke a wild magic in the plane, a crackling blue lightning the newcomers called thunder, and within a few years they were building it into rifles, pistols, crossbows and the cuffs that lawmen used to hold their prisoners.",
                        "Nobody governed the place. The town of Omenport grew up where an unusual number of Omenpaths opened in the desert, all faded paint and triangular rooftops, with mission bells at sundown and a fountain kept cool by magic. The wealth gathered in Prosperity, a city of whitewashed buildings and raised railways, owned in all but name by the Sterling Company of Baron Bertram Graywater. The Sterling Company was the closest thing Thunder Junction had to law, and it mostly protected its own money. Out past the rails rode the Hellspurs, a gang of fire and thunder bandits led by {Akul the Unrepentant}, a dragon built like a scorpion, with golden eyes, mandibles, two rows of teeth and a barbed tail he cracked like a whip.",
                        "Annie Flash knew exactly what Akul was. She had ridden with the Freestriders, a gang that once robbed the Hellspurs. Akul hunted them for weeks and killed whoever got in his way, and when the survivors tried to give back what they had stolen, he struck anyway and maimed her nephew. Annie quit, buried her thunder rifle under an unmarked stone a hundred paces from her fence, and took up ranching outside the remote town of Saddlebrush with her horned mount, {Fortune, Loyal Steed}. She had one gift she could not bury, an eye given to her by an angel that could see for miles and see straight through illusions.",
                        "One morning she rode past a carriage Akul had torn in half and set burning, its courier and guards dead in a ring of fire. The dragon had been after a package, and the package had been a decoy. Annie took the lockbox of cash the Hellspurs had left behind. When she got home, a well-dressed stranger was waiting on her porch. Her eye saw through his disguise at once. He was Oko, a fae shapeshifter who had kept his spark, and he was putting a gang together to rob Akul.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-gang", "The Gang",
                    listOf(
                        "Annie said no, and then she dug up her rifle, because if Oko could find her, so could the Hellspurs. She rode to the Wildcard Saloon in the dying ranch town of Rustwood and met the crew. There was Breeches, a goblin who blew things up, and Malcolm, a siren who watched from the sky. There was Tinybones, a skeleton thief who kept what he stole inside his own ribcage, and the necromancer siblings Gisa and Geralf, who fought each other as hard as anyone else. There was Eriette, a witch from Eldraine whose charms could turn a guard into a friend, and Rakdos, the demon lord of a Ravnican guild, too big to fit through the saloon door. Second in command was the gorgon Vraska, whom most of the Multiverse believed dead in the invasion.",
                        "Their client was the planeswalker Ashiok, a sculptor of nightmares, and the target was Maag Taranau, the legendary vault at the heart of the plane. Akul had built a whole town around it but could not get in. The key, or one piece of it, sat in Bertram Graywater's safe. Oko meant to steal it and break two of the crew, the warlock Kaervek and the ninja Satoru Umezawa, out of the Sterling cells beneath Prosperity on the same day.",
                        "It went smoothly. Eriette and Annie slipped in through a dissolved vent and charmed their way to the cells. Oko walked into headquarters disguised as a courier and had Tinybones delivered to Graywater's office in a box, and Umezawa opened the safe hidden behind a portrait. The key was small and blackened, gleaming in strange fluorescent spots. Then, on the road out of town, Oko passed Ral Zarek and his new bodyguard.",
                        "The bodyguard was Kellan, a half fae boy from Eldraine who had crossed from world to world looking for the father he had never met. His last employer on Ravnica had told him his father was on Thunder Junction, so he had signed on as a laborer building Ral's relay tower at Omenport, and after he caught a worker falling from the scaffold, Ral made him a guard. Father and son felt each other's fae magic, and the shock broke Oko's disguise in the middle of the street. Sterling guns opened up. Oko fled as an eagle, Kellan caught up with him, and Kellan told him who he was. Oko took him into the gang, not out of love, but because a boy who offered loyalty that freely looked useful.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-train", "The Train",
                    listOf(
                        "In the saloon Kellan recognised two faces he knew from trouble on Eldraine, Eriette and Ashiok. Oko told him grudges from other planes did not count on Thunder Junction. Kaervek studied the stolen key and found it was not Thran or Phyrexian, but as old as either or older, and he warned that it might be more than a key. Nobody knew where the vault actually was. Annie knew a scholar of ancient artifacts named Nolan, and the Sterling Company had hired him to study the key. He was riding a guarded train into Prosperity.",
                        "Oko promised Kellan that no innocents would be hurt. The plan was for Breeches to blow a bridge and stop the train while Gisa raised zombies aboard as a distraction, all of it coordinated through Ashiok's voice in their heads. Gisa raised her dead too soon. The zombies overran the cars and reached the driver, and instead of stopping, the train sped up toward the broken bridge. Oko and Kellan crossed the roof to the prisoner car and got Nolan out, and Annie rode alongside to haul the scholar and a wounded Umezawa onto Fortune's back.",
                        "Then Oko jumped, and told his son the passengers could not be saved. When Kellan refused to come, Oko rode away from the Sterling reinforcements without him. Kellan threw golden vines around the last car and held it while the front of the train hung out over the canyon. Annie came back for him. She held off the Sterling riders while the passengers climbed out, and then the train went over the edge and burst in the canyon below.",
                        "At camp, Oko said he had left because he trusted Kellan to finish the job. Ashiok drew Nolan's memories out of his head like silver threads. The key was only one of six pieces, and Akul wore the other five on a medallion around his neck. A map to his town, Tarnation, lay buried in a prospectors' graveyard called Thief's Folly. Gisa and Geralf came back bruised with the map, and it showed that the vault hung in the air over Akul's town.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tarnation", "Tarnation",
                    listOf(
                        "Tarnation sat in a circular canyon beneath a jagged floating mountain, with clouds circling its spire and lava pouring off its edges into rivers that ran through the streets. The houses were built of lava rock and sharpened timber and framed with animal bones. Disputes were settled by duels in the street, and the losers went into a fire pit. Oko, Vraska, Annie and Kellan walked in disguised, and within minutes Kellan had been too polite to a Hellspur and started a brawl. They were chained in an old quarry and brought before Akul, who took the sixth piece of the key and slotted it into his medallion. It grew a sixth spike and its last glass dome lit violet. It had always been one key.",
                        "With nothing left to lose, Kellan challenged the dragon to a duel. Akul laughed, until the prisoners called him a coward in front of his own gang, and then he had no choice. At midnight, under a molten clock tower, he ringed the dueling ground with fire and declared it a fight to the death. Kellan's spear bounced off his scales. Then the fae magic in Kellan woke properly for the first time, and he put the dragon into a trance and looped a vine around his throat. He could not bring himself to pull it tight. Akul broke free and pinned him.",
                        "Thunder struck the dragon before the killing blow. Ral Zarek and a Sterling army came over the ridge, and they had been sent there by Oko, who had tipped off Graywater before ever setting foot in Tarnation. He had brought Kellan along because he knew the boy would get them caught and bring them face to face with Akul and his key. Tinybones had ridden in disguised as the bones of Oko's armor, and now he put himself back together and unlocked the chains. Vraska turned the guards to stone. The medallion had been torn from Akul's neck in the chaos, and Kellan threw it to his father.",
                        "A blast threw Kellan down, and a Sterling guard clubbed him senseless. The last thing he saw was his father looking at him without interest, and then walking away toward the vault. He woke in a Sterling prison wagon. Ral broke him out with lightning and made him an offer. Whatever was in that vault, nobody on Thunder Junction could be trusted with it, and Ral meant to take it somewhere no one would ever find it. Kellan agreed.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The vault cracked open, and the unlikely alliance met the likeliest of ends, as greed, rage, and self-interest won out over any shred of loyalty.",
                        "Betrayal at the Vault",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-vault", "The Vault",
                    listOf(
                        "Annie's eye opened the round door of {Fomori Vault}, and the crew went to work on its defenses. Umezawa's paper drone and Tinybones slipped through a grid of deadly light, and Kaervek unpicked a ward. Akul arrived with what was left of the Hellspurs and a fresh gash across his face, and Rakdos threw himself at the dragon with delight. Oko and Vraska went on alone to the inner door. Kellan caught his father's wrist with a lasso, and Ral and Vraska fought, and Annie grazed Oko's shoulder on purpose. Then Ashiok arrived and dropped Kellan, Annie and Ral with nightmares, and Oko turned {The Key to the Vault} in the lock.",
                        "Inside was endless golden mist and floating stairs that overlapped and crossed. At the top stood a glass pod in a metal cage written over with ancient script, beneath a mural of a vast horned creature. Ashiok let the disguise fall, and it was not Ashiok at all. It was [Jace Beleren](who/jace), believed dead since the invasion, his arms scarred and plugged where the Phyrexian cables had been. He shattered the pod. Inside slept a small creature with orange fur, two dark horns and a hollow tail with a blue light flickering in its tip. Vraska told Oko his services were no longer needed and turned her gaze on him, and he planeswalked away with stone already climbing his neck. Jace and Vraska vanished with the child.",
                        "The vault began to come down around everyone else. Rakdos carried the crew out on his back. Akul seized Annie at the last moment, and Kellan stopped fighting what he was. He took the dragon's mind entirely and ordered him back into the vault, never to come out, and Akul walked in as the rubble sealed behind him. Then the mountain shed its stone. Underneath was a golden sphere, and it shot up into the clouds. Two weeks later it fell back and smashed across Tarnation. The story never confirms whether Akul survived.",
                        "The unpaid crew broke up at the Wildcard, promising to meet again for a bigger score or for revenge on Vraska. Oko planeswalked away and let Kellan's life be his own. Kellan stayed on at Annie's ranch and turned down Ral's offer of work on Ravnica. Through the relay tower he sent a message to Amalia, a cartographer he had met on Ixalan, and she arrived with ink pots and rolled maps to chart a plane nobody had charted yet. Ral went home to Ravnica with one thought he could not shake, and it would lead him to [Bloomburrow](bloomburrow).",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "As the vault collapsed around them, Kellan made sure Akul couldn't follow them out.",
                        "Getaway Glamer",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "vryn", "Vryn",
                    listOf(
                        "How Jace came to be in that vault began a year earlier, at the foot of the Invasion Tree in the last hours of the [invasion](invasion-of-the-multiverse). Nearly compleated, he had tried to set off the Filigree Sylex and end everything, and nothing had happened. He woke with Elspeth's sword through his chest and its Halo in his blood, which gave him back a little of himself. He hid behind an illusion, slipped into [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn)'s mind, and found her order for him. Go home. His Phyrexian body took him to Vryn, the world he had come from and had walled out of his memory, and there it killed Vryn soldiers by sending them into seizures.",
                        "From a safe pocket deep in his own mind, Jace fought back. He ordered his body to treat the oil as a sickness, burned it out with a fever, and tore the cables from his arms. Then he went to Ravnica, where the invasion was still raging, and found Vraska compleated on a rooftop, still holding on inside a hidden room he had built in her mind long before. He broke her free, walled the oil away from her, and carried her into the Blind Eternities to the one healer he had known all his life. They collapsed on an indigo rug in the house of his mother, Ranna Beleren, who had believed him dead for decades.",
                        "Ranna healed them over months. On Vryn the two of them saw an Omenpath for the first time and decided the Multiverse was too broken to fix. Repair was not enough, they agreed, and they began to talk of rebirth. Vraska tried to leave and found her spark was gone. Jace remembered an errand from nine years before, when [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas)'s servant Tezzeret had sent him to a sealed vault on an empty desert world, and he had sensed a sleeping, childlike mind inside it. Tamiyo had later told him there were other vaults like it, relics of an empire lost to history.",
                        "So Jace put on Ashiok's face. He freed Eriette from a jail on Eldraine, Vraska recruited Breeches and Malcolm on Ixalan, and Oko did the rest without knowing who had hired him. When Jace reached into the child's mind after the heist, he found its name was Loot, and that it held a living map of every world and the endpoint of every Omenpath, with planes being born and dying in real time. Vraska could travel again. The two of them promised to keep Loot safe. What exactly they meant to do with him, the story does not yet say.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction"),
                StorySource("Set", "The Big Score"),
                StorySource("Story", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction, Episodes 1 to 6, by Akemi Dawn Bowman, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Outlaws of Thunder Junction, Epilogues 1 and 2, Bring the End, by Alison Lührs, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Outlaws of Thunder Junction"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Maag Taranau"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Kellan"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Akul"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "bloomburrow", "Bloomburrow",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "valley", "Valley",
                    listOf(
                        "There are no humans on Bloomburrow. Its people are animals, mice and rabbits, frogs and badgers, bats, lizards, otters, rats and more, the true size of the creatures they resemble, and they wear clothes, bake bread, keep farms and work a magic they call weaving. Their home is Valley, a haven of meadows, ponds, marshes, hills and thickets ringed by high hills, with the wild Brambles beyond. Each people weaves in its own way. Mousefolk blur into speed. Frogfolk call up water and read the future. Batfolk read the stars, squirrelfolk raise the dead, lizardfolk work fire, and ratfolk keep the memory of everything.",
                        "The great danger of Valley is the Calamity Beasts. They are huge, wordless elementals, living seasons in the shape of animals. Some come on schedule, like the Spring Elk and the Sun Hawk, and the folk plan around them as they plan around rain. Others come when they please and wreck whatever lies in their path. In the deep past, Valley's stories say, the beasts brought storms and blizzards, wildfires and plagues, until the first weavers taught themselves magic to protect all animalfolk together.",
                        "The greatest of those defenders was Lily of the Valley, champion of the holly, who drove the beasts back with a burning sword called Cragflame. The tale says the blade was carved from the fang of the Wildfire Wolf that nearly destroyed Bloomburrow. The same great magic later drove some weavers mad, and the folk gave up the grand weavings for small spells that anyone could manage. They learned to live alongside the beasts, warning each other, dodging them and rebuilding after.",
                        "In living memory the Calamities had been rare. Then, on an ordinary midmorning beside a frogfolk village called Pondside, a young augur sat sketching by the water, and her pencil began to draw something on its own.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "pondside", "Pondside",
                    listOf(
                        "Helga was a small green frogfolk with amber eyes and a nervous smile, raised by grandparents who were now gone, and she had once been an apprentice to King Glarb of Fountainport, the greatest augur in Valley. She had not finished. Her neighbours thought her visions were daydreams. That morning her pencil drew a beast she had never seen, something like the Sun Hawk but with four wings stretched on bat-like fingers, a fin of a crest, a forked tongue and a storm at its back.",
                        "She was on her way to show it to the village augur when the watch bell rang. Over Pondside circled Maha, the Night Owl, a Calamity Beast as broad as an oak's branches, deep blue and violet, with turquoise magic burning in its eyes and talons. It made no sound. Behind it the day itself was cut away, and night followed in its wake, full of stars no one had seen at that hour. {Maha, Its Feathers Night} tore through the village, the cook fires caught, and by the time the owl was gone Pondside was burnt and splintered ruins.",
                        "Helga fell into Pond Creek in the chaos and came out alone, under a moonless sky with unfamiliar stars. She followed the creek, limping, toward the next town upstream. It was a mousefolk village called Goodhill, with roofs of white petals, walls of woven grass and batfolk houses perched high on poles.",
                        "In Goodhill it was a baker's birthday. Her children had strung up a banner, the mayor wanted to see the family relic, and a weaselfolk came running to say there was a stranger in the creek. The baker took up her rapier and went to look, and found a muddy frog who whispered that a Calamity Beast had come, and then fainted at her feet.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Its wingspan reaches from dusk to dawn.", "Maha, Its Feathers Night"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mabels-party", "Mabel's Party",
                    listOf(
                        "Mabel was a mousefolk baker with auburn fur, married to Clem, a plump grey baker, and mother to three children, Rosalyn, Foggy and Pip. As a girl she had been wild, climbing the Cliff of Heroes with other young mice and helping drive bandits away from Goodhill. Then she had chosen a quiet life, and found it enough. She was also, by family tradition, a descendant of Lily of the Valley, and up in her attic, under a sheet, stood a sword carved from a massive tooth, its blade etched with sigils, its pommel shaped like a flame.",
                        "Her mother had always doubted the old story about that sword, and said that a story did not need to be true to have power. When Helga told the town what had happened to Pondside, the mayor waved it away as imagination. Mabel believed her. That evening, at her own birthday party, among the strawberry cake and blackberry punch, she held the sword aloft and fire ran down its blade from hilt to tip. She said she was going to Pondside, and asked who would come.",
                        "Four answered. Finneas was a sable rabbitfolk farmer in a straw hat, banned from archery contests for winning too often, who had never been past the last farmstead. Hugs was a gruff old badger three times Mabel's height who let the children climb all over him. Gev was a lizardfolk with fire in his maces who went wherever Hugs went, the pair of them old rogues. Zoraline was a batfolk cleric troubled by what she saw in the stars, who slept on Hugs's back for much of the trip. And Helga came too, because it was her village. That made a party of {Mabel, Heir to Cragflame}, an archer, a badger, a lizard, a bat and a frog who did not trust her own magic.",
                        "Pondside, when they reached it, was a ruin with one door banging in the wind. Its survivors had fled south. Two squirrelfolk necromancers were picking through the wreck, and Mabel's party overheard them talk of somebody called Cruelclaw and of the last one, before the necromancers raised skeletons against them. The party won, and decided to follow where the trail led, up the Long River to the biggest city in Valley.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "three-tree-city", "Three Tree City",
                    listOf(
                        "Someone else had arrived in Valley by then, and he was furious about it. Ral Zarek had come home from [Thunder Junction](thunder-junction) knowing that [Jace Beleren](who/jace), mourned as dead for nearly two years, had been alive all along, disguised as Ashiok and stealing a strange animal from under Ral's nose. Ral tried to planeswalk to him and landed on a beach in Ixalan. The Wanderer of Kamigawa could not help, because her spark was gone. She told him to go back to where he had last seen Jace and open his soul.",
                        "In the ruins of Tarnation, Ral reached out with his storm magic and brushed the faint trace of a green aether trail, smudged as if someone had tried to wipe it away. He followed it into the Blind Eternities and came out in a field of dandelions taller than trees. Bloomburrow turns every visitor into one of its own peoples. Ral had become an otter, black furred and goggled, with a copper bracer on his arm and a tail that kept hitting people by accident. Card collectors know him in that shape as {Ral, Crackling Wit}.",
                        "The party met him in Three Tree City, a city grown into an oak, a sycamore and a willow twined together on the banks of the Long River, where a great quilted banner stood for the union of all the animalfolk. Ral was pestering the locals about a fox. Helga had already drawn that fox in her journal and dreamed of him, together with a lizard of green and black scales and a small creature too hard to see. Ral took her seriously and would not let her out of his sight. Then the Flood Gar, a silver fish of a Calamity, struck the docks, and the {Calamitous Tide} washed them all down a tributary, Hugs keeping everyone afloat.",
                        "They washed up among ratfolk lorekeepers living in tunnels dug long ago by giant insects. Their elder showed them a spell image of Cruelclaw, a weasel mercenary in a red hooded coat with three claw scars across one eye, and said her scouts had seen him carrying a Calamity Beast's egg. That was why Maha had come to Pondside. Mabel decided they would find the egg and give it back. Helga, told to stop forcing her magic, idly drew Fountainport. On the way there, in a dandelion field, the four-winged beast from her very first drawing came down on them in purple lightning.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "fountainport", "Fountainport",
                    listOf(
                        "Ral looked at it and saw a dragon, changed by the plane into a bird and not from Bloomburrow at all. Finneas put a copper-wired arrow into its beak, Ral sent lightning down the wire, and {Dragonhawk, Fate's Tempest} flew off wounded. Ral filed the strange beast away as something to warn people about. Dragons were slipping out of somewhere, and in time the whole Multiverse would learn where, in [Tarkir Dragonstorm](tarkir-dragonstorm).",
                        "Fountainport was a city carved as a three-tiered soapstone fountain rising from Valley's largest pond, with glass elevators, slides and waterfalls, and a spire of water hanging over the throne room. Helga had not been back since she was sent away in disgrace. King Glarb received them, and behind a screen he showed them the egg, velvet blue and flecked with glittering stars. {Glarb, Calamity's Augur} had paid Cruelclaw to steal it. He meant to raise the beast as Valley's own champion against the other Calamities, and he did not care what Maha burned while it searched for its child.",
                        "Glarb bound the party and summoned {The Infamous Cruelclaw} to finish them. Helga slid the weasel into the king with a spell, and the fight was on. Cruelclaw's rapier was poisoned. He called Mabel a farmer, and she corrected him that she was a baker, and thinking of her family she set Cragflame alight and disarmed him, then let him go and told him to choose better. Then the Dragonhawk dived on the city. Glarb fled through a portal. Mabel climbed Helga's water steps to the stone lily at the city's crown with her sword burning, and Maha came out of the dark, struck the Dragonhawk, ripped out a tail feather and drove it away.",
                        "The owl landed and looked at the mouse. Mabel carried the heavy egg to it, and Maha took it gently and flew off, and the night it left behind felt peaceful for once, a moment shown on {Dawn's Truce}. Goodhill threw the party a parade. Helga stayed on as the town's hedgewitch. Before Ral left, her eyes glowed and she prophesied that the kings in the dark would return and that the mage in blue would bring about the end. Mabel gave him two ironsap buttons stamped with holly leaves, one for him and one for his husband, and he vanished in a crackle of lightning, still no closer to Jace than when he started.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Bloomburrow"),
                StorySource("Story", "Bloomburrow, Episodes 1 to 5, by Valerie Valdes, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Bloomburrow"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Bloomburrow (plane)"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Mabel"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Maha"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "duskmourn", "Duskmourn",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "the-cherry-door", "The Cherry Door",
                    listOf(
                        "After the invasion, Nashi kept his mother close. Tamiyo, the moonfolk storyteller of Kamigawa, had been compleated and killed, and what remained of her lived on as a memory held in one of her own story scrolls. Nashi, the young nezumi she had adopted, carried that scroll with him across the [Omenpaths](the-omenpaths). Then the scroll vanished. Nashi told a few of his friends among the Reckoners that he could hear her calling, and three months later he was gone too, and four of those friends with him.",
                        "The Wanderer, Kamigawa's emperor, had lost her spark in the Desparkening and found peace in finally belonging to one world. She found the planeswalker Kaito Shizuki in the middle of robbing an imperial vault and told him the boy was missing. Kaito gathered a team. Tyvar Kell, the stone-skinned elf prince of Kaldheim, came for the glory of it, and brought Niko Aris, a former planeswalker from Theros who threw shards of light that never missed and who was bitter about the spark they had lost.",
                        "The trail led to Ravnica. In a quarantined courtyard, behind Azorius wards and a Boros evacuation notice, stood a door of stained cherry carved with moths and branches, standing on its own with nothing behind it. People had gone through it and not come back. Niv-Mizzet had his researchers swarming around it and attached one of his own to the rescue, Zimone Wola of Strixhaven, a brilliant dimensional scholar who had never been a planeswalker and thought the Omenpaths the beginning of a wonderful age. The detective Alquist Proft said flatly that the door had none of the marks of an Omenpath.",
                        "A young former planeswalker named Aminatou, who could twist fate, recoiled at the sight of it. Her magic and the door's repelled each other like oil and water, and she gave each of them a token of her power to cheat one terrible death. The Wanderer reached for the knob, and the door swung open on its own, onto a dusty foyer seen through a fuzz of blue light. {Enter the Enigma} shows her crossing. The door slammed behind the last of them.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Though she was used to stepping into the unknown, something about this strange doorway made the Wanderer shiver as she crossed the threshold.",
                        "Enter the Enigma",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-house", "The House",
                    listOf(
                        "Duskmourn had once been an ordinary world of cities, forests and two suns. On a quiet street, in a black-roofed house on the corner by the woods, one of its owners had summoned a demon to serve as a household spirit, and was horrified by what came. Too weak from the crossing to resist, the demon was bound into the structure of the house and buried beneath its basement. The owners died, the house changed hands a dozen times, and the thing below waited.",
                        "Then a family named Vendrell moved in with a lonely sixteen-year-old daughter, Marina, who found the presence in the basement and talked to it. It called itself Valgavoth, Val for short, and it became her only friend, teaching her demonology and necromancy. One day, after cruel treatment at school, she poured out her misery and he offered to fix it. She brought her tormentors down to the basement, and hands reached out of the walls and took them. {Demonic Counsel} shows him whispering in her ear.",
                        "After that the house grew. The homes on either side vanished into it, then the street, then the city, then other cities along the roads like blood along arteries, then the forests and finally the suns. Marina's parents disappeared. She threatened to burn the house down, and Valgavoth promised to give her back the world as she remembered it, and she believed him. In truth the House swallowed the entire plane. Every room of it is part of his body, and the floors and walls are his flesh. {Valgavoth, Terror Eater} feeds on fear, and for generations the survivors trapped inside had lived by an uneasy truce, keeping to safe rooms and the paths between them while the House farmed their terror.",
                        "The Omenpaths broke that truce. Opening a door to another world used to cost Valgavoth so much that he could manage one every few years. Now it was easy. New victims poured in, and the House no longer needed to keep anyone alive for later. It began to hunt. The rescue team walked straight into a House that was hungrier than it had ever been.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "split-apart", "Split Apart",
                    listOf(
                        "Inside, the rooms would not stay where they had been. Hallways changed behind them. A kitchen opened into a bedroom, a bedroom into a conservatory, and beyond a pair of glass doors an abandoned funfair loomed over a field of rotting corn that rustled with no wind to move it. Niko led them by keeping always to the right, a trick from the labyrinths of Theros. Then a scream drew Tyvar through a side door, and Zimone ran after him, and the doorway behind them became wall.",
                        "Kaito turned to follow and the floor opened beneath him like the mouth of a lamprey, ringed with teeth, and swallowed him. The Wanderer was left on her knees in front of smooth tile. Within minutes the House had taken three of them. Tyvar and Zimone found themselves in a vast library holding the lost knowledge of an entire world, and Zimone could not tear herself away, while Tyvar punched holes in the walls and learned to wrap them both in the House's own flesh so its monsters could not see them.",
                        "Niko and the Wanderer, crossing a cold room of hanging carcasses, were pulled aside by a gaunt young survivor in clothes stitched from a dozen sources, some of them wallpaper. He showed them a note asking for silence, then used a slingshot and a bear trap to bring down a masked razorkin, a survivor the House had remade into a butcher. His name was Winter. He knew the House, how to move through it and which things in it would not kill you, and he offered to guide them.",
                        "Kaito fell through a burning forest and a dead city where dragons wheeled, an echo of Duskmourn's distant past, and landed in a basement. There he punched a man who was creeping up behind him. It was [Jace Beleren](who/jace), whom Kaito had last seen trying to destroy everything at the Invasion Tree. Jace had come to the House looking for Vraska and their new companion, whom he had lost somewhere inside.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-cult", "The Cult",
                    listOf(
                        "The two planeswalkers fought their way down together. Jace unmade a nightmare by reaching into the minds trapped inside it and taking their fear away, because a nightmare cannot live without fear. Then the floor gave way into a pit of gel, and shackles closed on both their ankles and began to drag them under. Kaito's drone picked Jace's locks first. Jace reached back to help him, and then looked up and saw, projected on the ceiling, Vraska shielding a small orange creature from a pack of razorkin. He apologised, said the creature was too important, and climbed. Every rung he touched dragged Kaito deeper.",
                        "Kaito tore himself out of the House with his spark and landed back in the Ravnican alley, clutching a bloodstained splinter of wood. When he tried to planeswalk back, nothing happened, and for a moment he feared his spark had gone out like everyone else's. Aminatou told him it had not. The House itself was refusing him.",
                        "Winter led Niko and the Wanderer onward. In an outdoor room full of dancing wickerfolk they found Nashi at last, and {Shardmage's Rescue} shows Niko shattering the wicker things and pulling him clear. Then Winter led all three through room after room of trapped forest and thicket into an impossibly broad meadow under endless twilight, dotted with huts. Beneath it was a cavern chapel full of hanging chrysalises. There robed cultists, clean and well fed where every other survivor was ragged, were sealing a bound survivor into a cocoon. Winter bowed his head and did nothing as the cultists seized the others. Their leader blew a cloud of silver dust from his book, and everyone fell.",
                        "Meanwhile Zimone and Tyvar found the oldest rooms of the House, where a girl's diary told the whole story of Marina and Val. Zimone understood what the rituals in its margins meant. With the power of four lives, Valgavoth could stretch his reach again and again, until there was nothing outside him at all.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Valgavoth whispered into Marina's ear until he was all she could hear.",
                        "Demonic Counsel",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-threshold", "The Threshold",
                    listOf(
                        "Niko woke tied to a chair beside Nashi and the Wanderer, in front of a stone altar. The cultists set down a box, and from it rose the shade of Tamiyo, flickering, with her face turned away. Their leader explained the plan. The rescuers carried the dust of many worlds, worlds that had never known the threshold, and through them Valgavoth would find his way to every one. Then the demon himself came out of the shadows above, an enormous moth spun out of nightmare, his wings grown into the walls. He had promised Winter freedom, and Winter had paid for it. Four lives, and there were only three prisoners, because the fourth had been the best friend Winter had once told Niko about.",
                        "Valgavoth opened a door for him to the moonlit world of Innistrad. Before Winter could reach it, Tyvar came crashing through the cultists, laughing, and Zimone, turned into a thing of splintered wood by Tyvar's disguise, cut Niko loose with hinge-like fingers. The Wanderer took up her sword and dueled the demon on his own altar. Niko sealed Winter away from the door in a shard. A cultist drove a knife through Niko's eye into their brain, and the world snapped back like a bowstring, and Aminatou's token had spent itself and Niko was alive. Nashi reached for his mother's scroll, and Tamiyo stopped him. The cultists had stolen nearly every story she was made of. Only her love for him was left, and they had used it to lure him here. She asked him to let her go, so her life's work could not be turned to evil.",
                        "Valgavoth pinned the Wanderer and opened his mouth over her. Nashi pulled the scroll from the trap, and Tamiyo smiled at him and dissolved into moonlight. Then a katana glowing with blue light burst out through the demon's chest. Kaito had come back through a door of his own, an artificial Omenpath built from his spark, Proft's mind magic, Aminatou's fate twisting and the splinter of the House he had carried out, framed in triangles, dragons and hopeful moths. {Get Out} shows the moment. Kaito's thrown blades saved Tyvar from one last nightmare, and everyone ran for the light of Ravnica. Nashi laid his mother's empty scroll down in the passage between worlds and left it there.",
                        "In the wreck of the chapel, Valgavoth dragged himself down from the ceiling, bleeding. He reached into the attic and pulled out a shivering bundle of orange fur. Loot was his now, and Loot knew the way to every world. Then he found Winter cowering by the wall where his door had been, and promised him freedom of a kind, and melted into the ceiling with him. Some time later, in Proft's quiet office on Ravnica, a door carved with glaring moths began to form in the wall behind him. When he turned around, nothing was there.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Kaito's blade brought the light of another plane into Duskmourn, and with it, a brief chance to escape.",
                        "Get Out",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Duskmourn: House of Horror"),
                StorySource("Story", "Duskmourn: House of Horror, Episodes 1 to 6, by Mira Grant, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Duskmourn: House of Horror"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Duskmourn"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Valgavoth"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "aetherdrift", "Aetherdrift",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "the-aetherspark", "The Aetherspark",
                    listOf(
                        "While Phyrexians tore through Ghirapur, an inventor named Ishikari Bindra climbed the tower of her workshop on the edge of town. Her professors had blacklisted her and her peers had refused to vouch for her. She had built a net of hollowed scales meant to catch something out of the Blind Eternities while the invasion held them torn open, and on its golden lever she had engraved a motto telling herself never to let common sense stop her. She was no mage, and she knew it might kill her. She pulled the lever anyway, and it did.",
                        "What her net caught became {The Aetherspark}, an artificial planeswalker's spark held in an ornate vessel of chilled aether. It was found in her lab after the invasion and put on public display. By then the plane around it had changed. The old ruling Consulate had handled the invasion badly and shared out aether unfairly, and a nearly bloodless uprising called the Indigo Revolution swept it aside. A new elected assembly took its place, and its first act was to give the world a new name. The plane once called Kaladesh became Avishkar, a word meaning invention.",
                        "The [Omenpaths](the-omenpaths) had been generous to Avishkar. Ghirapur found itself ringed with stable paths to other worlds, and the new assembly set out to make the city the hub of the Multiverse, opening embassies on other planes and eyeing Ravnica as its great rival. To show the Multiverse what Avishkar could do, it staged a race. The first Ghirapur Grand Prix had been a chaotic dash across nearly two dozen worlds. The second ran a fixed course, out of Ghirapur through an Omenpath to Amonkhet, on to the primeval jungles of Muraganda and back again, and its prize was the Aetherspark itself, promised to the winner as the power to travel anywhere.",
                        "In a world where almost every planeswalker had lost their spark, that prize was worth more than gold. A great many people wanted it, and not all of them meant to win it on the track.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-starting-grid", "The Starting Grid",
                    listOf(
                        "The home team was the Aether Rangers, run by Pia Nalaar, a renegade turned revolutionary turned rebuilder. Her driver was a masked newcomer called Spitfire, who was really Sita Varma, the daughter of a wealthy family loyal to the old order, and Pia had fitted her with a brass suit holding three aether canisters, each worth about two seconds of slowed time. Pia had built that car and suit with someone else in mind. Her daughter [Chandra Nalaar](who/chandra) had come home, and then signed on to race for the reigning champions, Cloudspire of Kylem, instead of for her mother.",
                        "Chandra had a reason. Nissa Revane had lost her spark, and Chandra meant to win the Aetherspark so that Nissa could travel again and go home to Zendikar. She wore a pin Nissa had given her and touched it whenever things went wrong. On the grid beside her were the Champions of Amonkhet, the undead leonin Zahur and the former planeswalker Basri Ket in chariots drawn by zombie hippos, the goblin rocketeers of the inventor Daretti, the griffin riders of Alacria, the sharkfolk Keelhaulers and the war-chanting Endriders. Commentary came from a dapper floating eyeball named Vin.",
                        "Then there were the Speed Demons, out of [Duskmourn](duskmourn). Their driver was Winter, the survivor who had betrayed the House's last visitors, and he raced in a spectral car for a skinless demon who promised him freedom if he brought back the Aetherspark and the House if he failed. Caged on the car, so small he had to stand on his hind legs, was Loot, the child Valgavoth had stolen from Jace and Vraska. Loot knew where every Omenpath went, including ones nobody else could find, and Winter was using him to cheat.",
                        "Chandra heard Loot crying before the start. She confronted {Winter, Cursed Rider} and bent two bars of the cage with her heat to make the little creature a perch. Then a badly built viewing stand collapsed as the race began, and Spitfire burned her first canister to thread straight through the hole of its falling stairwell, and Avishkar had a new hero.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mohars-coup", "Mohar's Coup",
                    listOf(
                        "Away from the track, two other visitors had come to Ghirapur for the same prize. On the first day of the race, [Jace Beleren](who/jace) and Vraska broke into the house of Mohar Varma, Sita's father, a nobleman who still insisted on calling his world Kaladesh and called the new name an insult to the Consulate. Jace froze him where he stood and read his mind. Then he made an offer. He would help Mohar put the Consulate back in power with a bloodless revolution of their own, and Mohar's opponents would end up in prison.",
                        "Mohar gathered allies. He brought in a former consul named Harshad, and Rudra, a disgraced captain of the old guard, who warned that it might come to blood. Mohar answered that no revolution worth having had ever been bloodless. Their plan was a trap at the finish line, with the Consulate's guards seizing the course in front of the whole Multiverse as the winner crossed it.",
                        "Vraska was uneasy. She worried aloud about the ordinary people who would be hurt, and Jace told her none of it would matter in a couple of weeks. He never said what he meant by that. What he wanted was Loot, who had been taken from them in the House of Duskmourn, and the Aetherspark that might let Vraska travel on her own again, and he was willing to put a whole government back on its throne to get them.",
                        "Out on the course, meanwhile, Spitfire had taken second place on the first stage behind the Speed Demons, which made no sense to anyone, and the race had passed through its first Omenpath into the heat of Amonkhet, where huge statues of gods stood over the oases.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Jace knew that if he just had time to explain his plan, he would find support. Unfortunately, time was in short supply.",
                        "Unstoppable Plan",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "muraganda", "Muraganda",
                    listOf(
                        "Amonkhet was brutal. Endriders hurled flaming chainsaws, and one of Zahur's charioteers, a young man named Khuru, died taking a blow meant for his captain and was raised again on the spot. Winter burst into blue flame and his demon's claw knocked the Champions off the track. Chandra's fireball smashed into the Speed Demons and Loot yelped when it hit, and she was ashamed of herself. Then an Omenpath carried the field into Muraganda, a world of trees the size of buildings, rivers and boulders hanging in the air, and quakes when its moons fell.",
                        "Sita, sheltering from a moonfall, remembered the invasion on Avishkar, the family guards turned Phyrexian and a limb of the Invasion Tree crushing a neighbour's house. Then lizardfolk raiders on dinosaurs ambushed the racers, and one of them hooked Loot's cage clean off Winter's car. Chandra left the race to go after him. Sita drove up the spine of a dinosaur with a canister, and when Pia ordered her to turn back and help, Sita told her family had only ever slowed her down and switched off her radio.",
                        "At the raiders' camp Loot was sharing a cup of hot chocolate from Fiora with Daretti, the goblin rocketeer, who was a prisoner too and fully expected them both to be sacrificed. Chandra crept in, Pia arrived and flattened a raider, and they all escaped in Daretti's rocket car. Loot steered them straight at a tree. Its bark fell away to show a hidden Omenpath, and {Explosive Getaway} catches the moment the little creature decided he liked these rescuers much better than the last ones.",
                        "That night Sita watched Pia talking with the raised charioteer Khuru, who was only Chandra's age, and began to wonder what his death had been for. The rocket car, meanwhile, came out of Loot's shortcut far from the track, and the engine did not survive the landing.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ketramose", "Ketramose",
                    listOf(
                        "They crashed at an oasis on Amonkhet with a scorch mark behind them and the engine blown. Daretti, who lived with chronic pain, shared out his medicine. They rebuilt the car out of palm fibre and hippo bones, with Chandra as the welding torch. By the fire, Chandra admitted why she had left her mother's team and broke down over Nissa, and Pia told her that nothing they made was ever wasted.",
                        "In the night a god came and sat by the water. Chandra had believed [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) killed every god of [Amonkhet](amonkhet) but one. {Ketramose, the New Dawn}, a young lion-headed god, explained that wherever there was breath and water and people who faced the dawn, Amonkhet would have gods. He cooled his feet in the oasis, gave them directions back to the track, and asked only that they tell him about other worlds. Chandra started with Zendikar.",
                        "Then Loot told them the truth about the race. He knew where every Omenpath was, and Winter had forced him to show the secret ones, which was how the Speed Demons had kept landing ahead of everyone. Pia hotwired the rebuilt car, and with {Loot, the Pathfinder} giving directions, they took one more shortcut home.",
                        "Back on the track, Sita and Winter were side by side on the final straight into Ghirapur. Neither of them knew that Consulate guards were waiting at the finish line, or that Jace Beleren was already there.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-finish", "The Finish",
                    listOf(
                        "The trap closed. The track's golden safety barrier rose in front of the leaders, Sita's mask broke, and her father's men dragged her before him. Jace searched Winter's mind and took him prisoner as a loose end. Loot's shortcut dropped Chandra's car out of the sky over Ghirapur, where Consulate guards held the finish. A dragon came out of a storm and attacked the city, and Ghirapur's defenses rose to meet it. Jace seized the minds of the wrecked racers and turned them on Chandra, who would not burn them.",
                        "A guard put a knife to Pia's throat. Sita spent her last canister, the one she had saved to win, breaking one guard's arm and dropping the other, and the Nalaars and the Varma heir fought side by side while Daretti's drones buzzed overhead and Vin broadcast every second. Jace struck Chandra with a spell that left her bleeding from the eyes, and even he looked shaken by it. He took Loot. Elspeth, now an archangel, fought the dragon, and Nissa rode a wave of vines to Chandra's side and begged Vraska not to go. Vraska went. She and Jace vanished into the fog.",
                        "Winter crawled toward the line on a broken leg, and a door opened on the track and dragged him back into the House, screaming. The race was won by the Champions of Amonkhet, whose chariot had been rebuilt by hundreds of hands while Basri's sand shielded the crowd and turned to glass under the dragon's fire. {Zahur, Glory's Past} and his team lifted the Aetherspark together. Mohar Varma went to prison for trying to overthrow the government, and the coup he had promised to keep bloodless never became a revolution at all.",
                        "Elspeth healed Chandra and warned them that the dragon storm was spilling across the Omenpaths. Far away, on a cliff above the steppes of Tarkir, Jace held a sleeping, restless Loot and walked on ahead of Vraska, into the place where the storms were born and into [Tarkir Dragonstorm](tarkir-dragonstorm).",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Aetherdrift"),
                StorySource("Story", "Aetherdrift, Episodes 1 to 6, by K. Arsenault Rivera, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Aetherdrift, Parts 1 and 2, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Aetherdrift"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Avishkar"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "tarkir-dragonstorm", "Tarkir Dragonstorm",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "five-spirit-dragons", "Five Spirit Dragons",
                    listOf(
                        "Tarkir had already been remade once. In the story told in [Tarkir](tarkir), Sarkhan Vol went back a thousand years and saved the dying [Ugin](who/ugin), and history bent around him. The dragonstorms that Ugin's presence fed never stopped, and instead of a world of warring khans, Tarkir became a world ruled by five dragonlords, Dromoka, Ojutai, Silumgar, Kolaghan and Atarka, each with a clan of humans bowed beneath it. Narset of the Jeskai had been the favourite pupil of Ojutai, the Great Teacher, who asked for devotion and got it.",
                        "Under the dragonlords the clans paid dearly. Dromoka made the Abzan renounce their honoured dead. Silumgar's paranoid boredom drove the Sultai to invent endless new festivals to keep him entertained. Kolaghan split the Mardu. Atarka ate her way through the whisperers of the Temur until they were little more than a hunting pack. The Jeskai gave up their own traditions willingly, because Ojutai had a gift for making his path seem the only one.",
                        "Then Narset and the Temur seer Alniul found a crumbling scroll holding the Stormnexus Ritual. At the Crucible of the Spirit Dragon, with the dragonlords closing in, five rebel leaders offered the ritual their lives, and out of the storms came five spirit dragons, each shaped by a clan's greatest hope. {Call the Spirit Dragons} shows them taking form. The Abzan had Betor, kin to all, who honoured the dead their dragonlord had forbidden. The Sultai had Teval, fair and impartial. The Mardu had Neriv, the living banner of the horde. The Temur had Ureni, and the Jeskai had Shiko, shaped by Narset's own reverence for the truth.",
                        "The dragonlords fell or fled. The clans were restored under khans, and Narset, to her own discomfort, became waymaster of the Jeskai. Travelling storytellers now acted out the revolution in taverns, wearing papier mache masks of her face. She hated the mask. She also kept a list of reports, each worse than the last, and a fear that Tarkir had traded one evil for a worse one. The storms were getting wilder.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-storms-break-loose", "The Storms Break Loose",
                    listOf(
                        "The dragonstorms had always birthed dragons, but never like this. They grew stronger and more frequent until they were throwing out monsters faster than the clans could fight them, beasts stranger and hungrier than any dragon Tarkir had known. Narset worked out why. The storms had never passed a certain intensity while Ugin watched over them, and Ugin was gone, nobody knew where. And now the storms were spilling across the [Omenpaths](the-omenpaths), each beat of their dragons' wings tearing at the paths, carrying dragons to other worlds. Ral Zarek had already met one on [Bloomburrow](bloomburrow), and another had attacked Ghirapur at the end of the race told in [Aetherdrift](aetherdrift).",
                        "Elspeth Tirel came to find out why. She had held the Filigree Sylex as it detonated at the end of the Phyrexian invasion and carried its blast into the Blind Eternities, and she had come back as an archangel, glowing, serene and hard to read. She arrived at the Storm Crane Monastery, a ruined temple turned into a Jeskai skyship, and asked Narset for help, because Narset's spark had gone out with the others and she could no longer look for answers herself.",
                        "That night a dragon tore up through the hull of the ship into the school in its belly. Teachers stood between it and the children. One girl held a barrier across a collapsed classroom with five younger students behind her. Elspeth flew into the dragon's open mouth just as it drew breath to freeze them, braced herself in its throat and gave the monks their opening, and the beast fled bleeding. She came out red from head to foot. When she said the dragons would devour other worlds if no one acted, Narset agreed to come.",
                        "Their search led first to the Crucible of the Spirit Dragon, where the revolution had happened, and to someone who was already there trying to make it happen again.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "sarkhans-heart", "Sarkhan's Heart",
                    listOf(
                        "Sarkhan Vol had loved dragons all his life, and for years he had been one, able to take a dragon's shape at will. The Desparkening took his spark, and with it went his dragon form, and with that the madness he had suffered under [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) came back. He could not stop the rebels or save the dragonlords he revered. At the Crucible he was trying to rebuild the Stormnexus Ritual into something greater, a storm that would pour through the Omenpaths and turn the whole Multiverse into a realm of dragons.",
                        "Elspeth and Narset found him there, fevered and starving. He lunged at Elspeth and she put him down with one blow to the ribs, and when he rose to stab her in the back, Narset struck him down again, the moment shown on {Narset's Rebuke}. Elspeth told him that if he came after them again, he would die. They left him in the dirt, and he limped for hours to a hut on the cold edge of Mardu territory, too ashamed to go home.",
                        "There a Jeskai monk named Taigam found him. Taigam had been Narset's rival under Ojutai and had stayed loyal to the dragon until he was forced to renounce him, and he grieved Ojutai's fall the way Sarkhan grieved his own. He offered Sarkhan a way to fly again. Together they hunted a young dragon, and Taigam bound it with blue fire while Sarkhan cut it open and took its heart, a blasphemy against everything he had worshipped. {Sarkhan's Resolve} catches him throwing his doubts aside.",
                        "The spell was the Stormnexus Ritual. The five khans had paid for it with their lives. Sarkhan paid with what he loved most, his reverence for dragons, and the corrupted ritual gave him back a dragon's body and more than he had bargained for. The dragon's heart screamed in his chest day and night. And every dragon the storms produced now answered to him.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "With Taigam whispering encouragement in his ear, Sarkhan threw aside his misgivings. The ritual would return him to his former glory. All he needed was a dragon's heart.",
                        "Sarkhan's Resolve",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-meditation-realm", "The Meditation Realm",
                    listOf(
                        "Elspeth and Narset went looking for Ugin. They were captured by the Abzan along the way, which Elspeth treated as a delay and Narset as a chance to win allies, and in Abzan lands Elspeth found her old friend Ajani, guilt-stricken over what he had done while compleated, who refused to come with her. Then a voice guided them deep into the storms, to an ancient temple nobody in Tarkir's histories recorded, and to an Omenpath like none they had seen, ragged at the edges like a bite torn out of the world. They stepped through into the Meditation Realm, the elder dragons' own plane of endless stairs and mirrored water.",
                        "[Jace Beleren](who/jace) was already there, with Loot asleep in his arms. Loot's mind had shown him the way. Elspeth had last seen him with her sword through his chest. Then the realm reshaped itself and they found what had kept Ugin from Tarkir. At the end of the [War of the Spark](bolas-war-of-the-spark), Ugin had sealed his brother here, and most of the Multiverse believed Bolas dead. He was alive, without his memories, and Ugin had made the realm a prison for both of them, meant to last until Bolas died millennia from now. Guarding him had worn Ugin thin.",
                        "Bolas did not know them, but something in him remembered Jace as an accomplice. While everyone stared, Jace's illusion dissolved and the real Jace seized the great gem between Ugin's horns. He swore he did not want to free Bolas. He had learned from Loot that the Meditation Realm touched every world and could be shaped by will alone, and with it he meant to fix everything, above all to undo what the Phyrexians had done to Vraska. Vraska herself stepped out of hiding and begged him to stop. He did not.",
                        "For a moment the realm swelled with every lost future, dead friends alive and worlds unbroken. But Jace was only human. The power burned through him like a wick, the horizon broke into mirrored shards over a pouring void, and as it rolled over him Jace shattered like glass. Bolas's chains came undone, and he rose laughing. Narset ran with a wailing Loot in her arms, Elspeth carried her through the Omenpath, and they came out of the temple under a sky black with dragons.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The Meditation Realm is a willful puzzle. Seek, and it will impede. Wait, and it will permit passage.",
                        "Stillness in Motion",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "ghostfire", "Ghostfire",
                    listOf(
                        "Sarkhan had come to destroy the temple and the Omenpath inside it, the only power on Tarkir that rivalled his control of the storms. Vraska vanished with Loot under a camouflage spell. Elspeth flew up alone to meet the great dragon and his horde, and Narset, waymaster of the Jeskai, refused to let her face them alone and stood on the temple platform throwing dragons into one another with her bare hands. Elspeth's task was not to win. It was to buy time, and she expected to die doing it.",
                        "Ajani changed his mind. He came down out of the sky with his axe raised, leaping from dragonback to kill a nightmare creeping up on Narset, and behind him came the dragons of the Abzan, fighting as one beast, a charge remembered on {United Battlefront}. Ajani tried to talk to Sarkhan, reminding him that long ago on Alara it was Sarkhan who had taught a newly sparked Ajani that rage and grief both had their place. For a moment Sarkhan hesitated with Elspeth caught in his jaws. Then he declared that the Multiverse deserved to burn for what it had taken from him, and lunged for her again, and Ajani caught her as she fell.",
                        "The wild dragons kept coming, and the Abzan began to die. Narset, Elspeth and Ajani were lining up to go for Sarkhan's eyes, knowing what it would cost, when a voice filled their bones and told the storm it was enough. Ugin had come out of the broken realm, bloodied and exhausted, and the wild dragons fled shrieking. Sarkhan screamed that Ugin had tormented him enough and poured fire over him. Ugin looked at him with sorrow and something like shame, and answered with ghostfire, colourless and without heat, and Sarkhan's scales burned to char. He dived away through the storm, burned but not dead.",
                        "Ugin admitted the storms had run wild without him to keep them in line. Then he told them the worst of it. He had failed to keep his brother. [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) was free somewhere in the Multiverse, and the man who had freed him was gone. Loot, who knew every road between worlds, was somewhere in the storm with Vraska. The oldest villain in the Multiverse had every road open to him, and nobody yet knew where he would go first.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The Abzan forces rallied to the call, driving back Sarkhan's feral dragon horde.",
                        "United Battlefront",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Tarkir: Dragonstorm"),
                StorySource("Story", "Tarkir: Dragonstorm, Episodes 1 to 7, by Cassandra Khaw, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Tarkir: Dragonstorm"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Sarkhan Vol"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Dragonstorm Arc"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "edge-of-eternities", "Edge of Eternities",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "sothera", "Sothera",
                    listOf(
                        "Beyond every world the planeswalkers knew lies the Edge, a single vast plane of billions of stars, wrapped like a ring around the outside of the Multiverse. Its people call it simply space. Looking inward, their instruments end at a scrambled barrier they call the Chaos Wall, and none of them know that everything else lies on the far side of it. Travel between its stars runs on eternity columns built by Pinnacle, a civilisation without money that was given the secret of faster than light travel by the Drix, a mysterious people who can walk between stars on their own. In return, Pinnacle promised to come whenever a star was dying and help the Drix search the wreck.",
                        "The star Sothera was dying. It had strayed into a cloud of interstellar aether and gorged itself until it was due to explode, and Pinnacle came, and so did everyone hoping to win the reputation that stood for wealth in a moneyless galaxy. The Kav were already there, a people who had once mined their own homeworld Kavaron so deep that they tore it in two. Eumidians gardened a frozen world. And two great faiths went to war over the star itself.",
                        "The Monoists, worshippers of a falling god they called INEVITA, did what nobody else had dared. Instead of letting Sothera explode, they collapsed it into a supervoid, a black hole ringed with violet light, and their faithful began to throw themselves into it, a holy plummet into what they believed was the Next Eternity. Against them came the Sunstar faith, whose Solar Knights reckon goodness on a cosmic ledger called the Sum, escorting the Dawnsire, a needle of a ship built to drive a horizon javelin into the dead star and light it again.",
                        "Somewhere beneath all of this, in the clay of a small world called Sigma, something old was waiting. It did not think of itself as a person. It thought of itself as a switch in a vast railyard, where every track ends at the same place but there are many ways to get there, and it had been lost a long time. It wanted to be found by the right people. Specifically, it did not want to be found by a man from somewhere else entirely, a man made of metal, who had arrived in the Edge out of the [Blind Eternities](the-blind-eternities) and quickly become one of the most feared criminals in Sothera.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("The future is set. The present remains uncertain.", "The Endstone"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "sigmas-reach", "Sigma's Reach",
                    listOf(
                        "The metal man was Tezzeret, once the master of the Infinite Consortium and a servant of [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas), whose body had been rebuilt in darksteel. Washed ashore in the Edge, he had made himself a power by fencing stolen goods and cornering the ice trade, and his Illvoi adviser Mm'menon had led him to strange readings around Sigma. Then Tezzeret noticed something odd. His organisation had no specialists in recovering dangerous artifacts, which was exactly the kind of mistake he did not make. The specialists he must have sent had simply been edited out of existence. So he sent the most disposable people he employed, and let whatever was on Sigma choose to be found by them.",
                        "Those people were Sami and Tannuk, the last crew of the Seriema, a battered torch ship rotten with neutron decay and twelve years overdue for a rebuild. Sami was a human captain whose crew had quit one by one, because Sami spent every job looking for their cat. Mirri had been startled one day, done what cats in the Edge do and slipped through space, and never come back. Tannuk was a Kav, huge, kind and plain-spoken, who had once commanded the great defensive lasers of Kavaron and chosen to destroy a falling mountain headed for a city of four million, then left his world afterward wanting to die. He met Sami on the wreck of a lost science ship called the Wurm Speaker.",
                        "Sigma's Reach was a mining colony that had never had any colonists. The power was on. Food sat warm on buffet trays in the dining hall, and empty slipsuits lay on the ground as if a hundred people had evaporated out of them. In the centre of the colony, Sami ran through a field of glass flowers, the frozen marks of an orbital bombardment that had stopped in the middle of a second. In a containment shed, where the mining machines tossed anything they could not process, lay a black stone. {Sami's Curiosity} shows Sami reaching for it while Tannuk's gut warned against it.",
                        "Nobody yet called it the Endstone. It could not change the present. What it could do was revise the past, so that the present it wanted came about anyway, and the bombed colony and its vanished miners were the leftovers of revisions nobody remembered. Sami sealed it in a stasis cask and set course for Tezzeret, with a stop at Kavaron on the way. Elsewhere, a young man was about to fall toward a star.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Tannuk's gut warned of danger, but Sami forged ahead without heed.", "Sami's Curiosity"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-dawnsire", "The Dawnsire",
                    listOf(
                        "Alpharael was a Monoist of Susur Secundi, the innermost world, a pleasure-loving youth who asked too many questions. His twin sister had already taken the plummet into the supervoid. He had been chosen as a kamu-shiku, a deleter, one of a suicide cadre that would ride a silent boarding pod called an inevitor into the Dawnsire. The night before, partying with miners, he pushed his hand through a strange thread of what they called no-hair, and it left a hole in his palm that could pass through solid things. {Alpharael, Dreaming Acolyte} shows him eager to prove himself worthy.",
                        "On the Dawnsire, Haliya was a squire to the Solar Knight Syr Vondam of the Sunstar Free Company, and she loved him the way a squire loves the knight who made her. The Monoist pod struck the ship and the cadre fought its way in, dying one by one, collapsing their own bodies into tiny voids. One of them tried to kill Vondam. A sliver of black hole passed through the knight's skull, and impossibly, he lived. The Dawnsire's captain had foreseen the moment as inevitable, and mercy let one of the attackers off the ship alive.",
                        "Vondam understood it at once. The odds had been loaded. A Monoist throwing away his place in paradise to kill a knight, succeeding, and surviving it was too much luck to be luck. Something wanted that Monoist alive, and if something could bend the past around Alpharael, it could have bent Vondam too. The knight called himself anstruth, a living blasphemy against reality, no longer quite the man he had been.",
                        "While the Dawnsire's great lasers fired across sixty million kilometres and broke open the Monoist monastery in orbit over Susur Secundi, a stolen Hopelight warship burned away from the battle, headed anywhere but home. Alpharael was aboard it. He would come down on Kavaron, close to a small, battered ship with a black stone in its hold.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "kavaron", "Kavaron",
                    listOf(
                        "Kavaron was two worlds now. Kavaron Before kept an atmosphere and days and nights under a sky constantly scratched by falling stone. Kavaron That Is was the rubble pile of the other half, pounded by everything the great defensive guns pushed away. The Seriema limped toward the frontier camp of Taro-duend carrying two dozen Kav soldiers dying of radiation, burned when Alpharael's warship came down on top of them. Sami had pulled the Monoist out of his wreck and shut him in the stasis cask with the stone, and he had a fit the moment it touched him and heard a voice.",
                        "The stone had already used him. The Kav had come to the Seriema as captors. The stone revised the path of the Hopelight's descent so that they had come as dying men instead, a revision it would count as its twelfth. Behind them, a Sunstar palestar arrived over Taro-duend carrying Vondam and Haliya. Vondam had opened a holy text written in blood, the anathalmanac, which held protocols for exactly this object. Protocol seven said that if an enemy of the faith touched it, everyone he had ever met, everyone the stone might rewrite, had to die. Every Kav in Taro-duend, down to the beasts of burden.",
                        "Haliya refused. She went down alone, armored against the stone with a swinging, chaotic pendulum that made her movements impossible to copy, and found the Seriema. She killed Sami and Tannuk. Then she had not. Alpharael pushed a jet injector through the hole in his hand and through her armor, a paralytic Sami had labelled as seizure medicine, and the stone undid the rest. When Haliya's armor purged the drug, she was a prisoner aboard a fleeing ship, and the palestar's lasers were killing everything in Taro-duend.",
                        "She radioed her knight that the object was aboard with her and begged him to stop. He would not. She begged him to kill her too, since the Sum demanded death for everyone near the stone. He would not do that either. Tannuk's old codes turned Kavaron's own guns to push the Seriema's sail out past the falling ring of stone into the dark. Sami declared everyone aboard a guest under mallowmass, the Drix law of hospitality, which was the only thing that stopped the squire and the Monoist from killing each other.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "infinite-guideline", "Infinite Guideline",
                    listOf(
                        "Haliya now had a question she needed answered, whether the fault for Taro-duend lay with Vondam, with the Sum, or with her. Alpharael drank and talked to someone who was not there. Sami and Tannuk tried to fix a fusion drive that could not be fixed. Then a cold black urchin of a ship with no engines grappled onto the Seriema out of nowhere, and Tezzeret pushed his way aboard. He had come for his stone. He repaired their drive, and the Endstone, as the crew began to call it, chose their course by letting a crystal fall onto a map. It pointed toward Infinite Guideline, Pinnacle's great station at the foot of the eternity column out of Sothera.",
                        "Both faiths followed. Monoist and Sunstar warships fell on the station from every direction, racing each other at hundreds of kilometres a second. Pinnacle defended itself, and its fighting machines poured out to meet them, and the battle killed thousands. Then something worse came. Inside the station, strange things from higher dimensions ate their way through the hulls, devouring metal and flesh and leaving lattices of white behind. Sami found Mirri there, safe in a forgotten stasis cask after years, and lost her again in an instant when the monster's noise startled her away.",
                        "A Drix named Barrow killed the creature and warned Sami and Tannuk that they were dusted in potential matter, which drew the hunters it called the eld. Elsewhere on the station, Vondam caught up with Haliya. He told her the protocol had made him do it, that its purpose was to make every knight act the same, so the stone could not swap a zealous knight for a merciful one. She answered that the fault then lay in the Sum. With Alpharael at her back, she reached through the hole in his hand into Vondam's spine and cut him away from his armor, and then, against his pleading, refused to kill him.",
                        "Tezzeret crushed a Monoist paladin who had boarded the Seriema. The crew opened the stasis cask to use the stone one more time, and out of the dark behind it trotted Mirri, who had walked herself to the safest place she knew. Sami wept. Then Sami rammed the ship straight at Infinite Guideline until the column had no choice but to open, and the Seriema fell into warp.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-last-thing", "The Last Thing",
                    listOf(
                        "In the galley, with coffee Sami had been saving for a special day, Tezzeret raised a toast to the spark in his chest and the unknown made known. Haliya reminded him that thousands had died. He said the dead had chosen their fights. The Endstone sat on the table and rolled until it pointed at him. It had lost its facets and looked like an egg. Tezzeret explained the trick. He had chosen the most hopeless crew he had so that they could succeed only by the stone's favour, and he had genuinely intended to take it wherever it wished to go. He wanted to find whatever had made it, and decide whether that maker was to be fled, bargained with or overthrown.",
                        "Sami had been studying their course. It ran out through the burning gas around the supervoid toward the Wurmwall, the wild edge of the system, to the place where the Wurm Speaker had died. Then the engine stopped. Something closed around the ship like a hand. The cosmos split open in the middle of the galley, and through it stepped a beautiful figure with a face like a mask of ancient green metal and nothing behind it but pale light.",
                        "Alpharael grabbed the Endstone and it stung him like a wasp, and it fell into the figure's hand. The figure touched his face, called him blessed, and he dropped senseless. Haliya cut two of its fingers off and it crushed her throat. It touched Tezzeret's chest, said he smelled of aether and tasted of the Wall, and seemed curious. It told Sami it was the last thing, and so the stone had come to it. It said the serpent needed a hero, and dared Sami to find it some day and put a lance through its heart. Then the stars closed over it and it was gone, the Endstone with it. {Temporal Intervention} catches the moment it arrived.",
                        "Haliya would live, and so would Alpharael. Tezzeret stood holding the two severed fingers, violet light leaking from them like blood, and smiled the way a hungry animal smiles. The Edge, he said, did have something like a planeswalker after all. Sami picked up Mirri, who was staring at the place where the universe had opened, and held her for a moment. Whatever came next, one thing was all right.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("It will not end any other way than this.", "Temporal Intervention"),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Edge of Eternities"),
                StorySource("Story", "Edge of Eternities, Episodes 1 to 11, by Seth Dickinson, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Edge of Eternities, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Edge of Eternities"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: The Edge"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Endstone"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Tezzeret"),
            ),
        ),
    ),
)
